---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { PLATFORM_KEYS, PLATFORMS } from '../lib/platforms';

const platforms = PLATFORM_KEYS.map((key) => PLATFORMS[key]);
const description =
  'Clipwell is a focused public-media downloader built around honest formats, clear limits and temporary processing.';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="About Clipwell"
  description={description}
  path="/about/"
  sectionLabel="The project"
  type="AboutPage"
>
  <p>
    Clipwell is a small, deliberate web utility for saving public video, audio and captions. It
    supports seven platforms: Instagram, Facebook, YouTube, TikTok, Kick, Twitch and X. It does not
    require an account, and it does not pretend to reach content that sits behind a login.
  </p>

  <p>
    The project began with a simple frustration: most downloader sites are either confusing walls of
    buttons or they promise formats the source never had. A 4K badge on a 480p video is not a
    feature. It is a broken expectation. Clipwell is built to read the public source, show the real
    options, and give a useful explanation when the platform says no.
  </p>

  <h2>What the product believes</h2>

  <p>
    A downloader should be quick to leave. There is no feed, no media library and no reason to create
    an account. The interface shows one link, one result and the formats attached to that result.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Real availability beats a long menu.</strong> Quality options are generated from the source, not a fixed marketing list.</li>
    <li><strong>Public means public.</strong> Private posts, login walls, age gates and DRM-protected media are not supported.</li>
    <li><strong>Failure should be specific.</strong> Bot checks, region locks, expired posts and unsupported URLs get different messages because they require different actions.</li>
    <li><strong>Data should not linger.</strong> Download files are held in temporary storage and removed automatically.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the service is built</h2>

  <p>
    The frontend is a statically generated Astro site with React only where interaction is needed.
    The downloader API is a separate FastAPI service that validates links, enforces rate limits and
    calls <code>yt-dlp</code> for public metadata and media. FFmpeg handles audio extraction and
    container preparation. The two halves can be deployed and scaled independently.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keeping the API separate is practical, not ornamental. It lets the public site stay fast and
    cacheable while the processing service carries its own concurrency, file-size and cleanup limits.
    It also makes the security boundary easier to reason about: the browser never receives backend
    credentials, and the backend only accepts hostnames on a small allowlist.
  </p>

  <h2>What Clipwell does not do</h2>

  <p>
    Clipwell does not bypass DRM, authenticate as you, accept session cookies, download private
    content, record live streams, strip watermarks or archive chat. It does not claim to be affiliated
    with any platform. It does not offer legal permission to reuse someone else’s work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those limits are part of the design. A tool that quietly crosses them would be harder to trust
    and harder to operate responsibly. If you need content you can only reach with an account, use
    the platform’s own features or contact the rights holder.
  </p>

  <h2>Platforms covered</h2>

  <ul>
    {platforms.map((platform) => (
      <li>
        <a href={`/${platform.slug}/`}>{platform.name} video downloader</a> — public links only.
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Contact and corrections</h2>

  <p>
    Platform behaviour changes frequently. If a guide is out of date or a public link fails in a way
    the error message does not explain, write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a> with the link, the platform, the
    exact message and the time of the attempt. Do not send passwords, cookies or private media.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
